<p id="mwEA"><b id="mwEQ">Quotation marks</b><sup about="#mwt16" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-altnames_3-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"altnames","group":"upper-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-altnames-3"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"group":{"wt":"upper-alpha"},"name":{"wt":"altnames"},"1":{"wt":"Also known as &apos;&apos;&apos;quotes&apos;&apos;&apos;, &apos;&apos;&apos;quote marks&apos;&apos;&apos;, &apos;&apos;&apos;speech marks&apos;&apos;&apos;, &apos;&apos;&apos;inverted commas&apos;&apos;&apos;, or &apos;&apos;&apos;talking marks&apos;&apos;&apos;.&lt;ref>{{cite book|title=100 Skill-Building Lessons Using 10 Favorite Books: A Teacher&apos;s Treasury of Irresistible Lessons &amp; Activities That Help Children Meet Learning Goals In Reading, Writing, Math &amp; More |last=Lunsford |first=Susan |publisher=Teaching Strategies |date=2001 |page=10 |isbn=9780439205795}}&lt;/ref>&lt;ref>{{cite book|title=Language Toolkit for New Zealand 2 |last=Hayes |first=Andrea |date=2011 |publisher=[[Cambridge University Press]] |page=17 |isbn=9781107624702}}&lt;/ref>"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-altnames-3" data-mw-group="upper-alpha" id="mwEg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFA">[</span>A<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFQ">]</span></span></a></sup> are <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punctuation" title="Punctuation" id="mwFg">punctuation</a> marks used in pairs in various <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Writing_system" title="Writing system" id="mwFw">writing systems</a> to identify <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct_speech" title="Direct speech" class="mw-redirect" id="mwGA">direct speech</a>, a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quotation" title="Quotation" id="mwGQ">quotation</a>, or a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phrase" title="Phrase" id="mwGg">phrase</a>. The pair consists of an opening quotation mark and a closing quotation mark, which may or may not be the same <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glyph" title="Glyph" id="mwGw">glyph</a>.<sup about="#mwt35" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-4"}}'><a href="#cite_note-4" id="mwHA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwHQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHg">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHw">]</span></span></a></sup> Quotation marks have a variety of forms in different languages and in different media.</p>

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" id="mwIA" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwIQ" aria-labelledby="History"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="History">History</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Quotation_mark&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1" title="Edit section: History"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt36" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwIg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"More citations needed section","href":"./Template:More_citations_needed_section"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"May 2026"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1364180890" about="#mwt37" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Message box/ambox.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><table class="box-More_citations_needed_section plainlinks metadata ambox ambox-content ambox-Refimprove" role="presentation" about="#mwt36"><tbody><tr><td class="mbox-image"><div class="mbox-image-div"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Question_book-new.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Question_book-new.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/99/Question_book-new.svg/60px-Question_book-new.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/99/Question_book-new.svg/120px-Question_book-new.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="icon" data-file-width="512" data-file-height="399" data-file-type="drawing" height="39" width="50" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div></td><td class="mbox-text"><div class="mbox-text-span">This section <b>needs <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Verifiability" title="Wikipedia:Verifiability">more citations</a></b>.<span class="hide-when-compact"> Please help <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Quotation_mark" title="Special:EditPage/Quotation mark">improve this section</a> by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Referencing_for_beginners" title="Help:Referencing for beginners">adding citations to reliable sources</a>. Unsourced material may be challenged and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Verifiability#Burden_of_evidence" title="Wikipedia:Verifiability">removed</a>.</span>  <span class="date-container"><i>(<span class="date">May 2026</span>)</i></span><span class="hide-when-compact"><i> (<small><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Maintenance_template_removal" title="Help:Maintenance template removal">Learn how and when to remove this message</a></small>)</i></span></div></td></tr></tbody></table><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt36"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_needing_additional_references_from_May_2026"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_needing_additional_references"/></span>
<p id="mwIw">The single quotation mark is traced to Ancient Greek practice, adopted and adapted by monastic copyists. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isidore_of_Seville" title="Isidore of Seville" id="mwJA">Isidore of Seville</a>, in his seventh-century encyclopedia, <span title="Latin-language text" about="#mwt38" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwJQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"la"},"2":{"wt":"[[Etymologiae]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="la"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etymologiae" title="Etymologiae">Etymologiae</a></i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Latin-language_text" about="#mwt38" id="mwJg"/>, described their use of the Greek <i id="mwJw">diplé</i>, a symbol like a right <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angle_bracket" title="Angle bracket" class="mw-redirect" id="mwKA">angle bracket</a>:</p><!-- not a chevron, which is a largely horizontal mark -->

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt39" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwKQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"verse translation","href":"./Template:Verse_translation"},"params":{"language":{"wt":"la"},"1":{"wt":"\n[13] ⟩ Diple. Hanc scriptores nostri adponunt in libris ecclesiasticorum virorum ad separanda vel [ad] demonstranda testimonia sanctarum Scripturarum.&lt;ref>{{cite magazine |url= https://www.lexpress.fr/culture/deux-points-et-guillemets-le-proces-verbal_779087.html |title=Deux-points et guillemets: Le &apos;procès-verbal&apos; |first=Pedro |last=Uribe Echeverria |date=7 August 2009 |access-date=5 June 2020 |work=[[L&apos;Express]] |lang=fr |quote=Dans le chapitre sur les symboles graphiques, Isidore évoque la diplè (chevron, en grec) : &apos; > Diplè : nos copistes placent ce signe dans les livres des gens d&apos;Eglise pour séparer ou pour signaler les citations tirées des Saintes Ecritures.&apos;}}&lt;/ref>&lt;ref>{{Cite book |title=Etymologiarum libri XX/Liber I |url= https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Etymologiarum_libri_XX/Liber_I#XXI.&lt;!--Yes, the trailing \".\" is part of the URL.--> |access-date=28 October 2020 |via=Wikisource |archive-date=31 October 2020 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201031113634/https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Etymologiarum_libri_XX/Liber_I#XXI.&lt;!--Yes, the trailing \".\" is part of the URL.--> |url-status=live}}&lt;/ref>\n\n"},"2":{"wt":"[13] ⟩ [[Diple (textual symbol)|Diplé]]. Our copyists place this sign in the books of the people of the Church, to separate or to indicate the quotations drawn from the [[Holy Scripture]]s.\n"}},"i":0}}]}'><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1319360790" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt40" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Verse translation/styles.css"}}'>.mw-parser-output .verse-translation{margin-left:1em}.mw-parser-output .verse-translation-head{text-align:center;font-weight:bold}.mw-parser-output .verse-translation-blockquote blockquote{margin:0;padding:0;border:0}.mw-parser-output .verse-translation-attr{font-size:90%}@media(min-width:640px){.mw-parser-output .verse-translation-blockquote{display:table-cell;vertical-align:top}.mw-parser-output .verse-translation-blockquote.first{padding-right:2em}}</style></span><div class="verse-translation" about="#mwt39" id="mwKg">
<div class="verse-translation-blockquote first"><blockquote><div style="font-style:roman;text-align:left" lang="" class="poem" typeof="mw:Extension/poem" about="#mwt51" id="mwKw" data-mw='{"name":"poem","attrs":{"style":"font-style:roman;text-align:left","lang":""},"body":{"extsrc":"\n[13] ⟩ Diple. Hanc scriptores nostri adponunt in libris ecclesiasticorum virorum ad separanda vel [ad] demonstranda testimonia sanctarum Scripturarum.{{#parsoid\u0000fragment:19}}{{#parsoid\u0000fragment:20}}\n\n"}}'><p id="mwLA">[13] ⟩ Diple. Hanc scriptores nostri adponunt in libris ecclesiasticorum virorum ad separanda vel [ad] demonstranda testimonia sanctarum Scripturarum.<sup about="#mwt43" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-5"}}'><a href="#cite_note-5" id="mwLQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwLg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLw">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt48" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-6" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-6"}}'><a href="#cite_note-6" id="mwMQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwMg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMw">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNA">]</span></span></a></sup><br id="mwNQ"/></p>
</div></blockquote></div>
<div class="verse-translation-blockquote second"><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1152813436" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt52" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Screen reader-only/styles.css"}}'>.mw-parser-output .sr-only{border:0;clip:rect(0,0,0,0);clip-path:polygon(0px 0px,0px 0px,0px 0px);height:1px;margin:-1px;overflow:hidden;padding:0;position:absolute;width:1px;white-space:nowrap}</style><span class="sr-only">Translation:</span><blockquote><div style="font-style:roman;text-align:left" lang="en" class="poem" typeof="mw:Extension/poem" about="#mwt53" id="mwNg" data-mw='{"name":"poem","attrs":{"style":"font-style:roman;text-align:left","lang":"en"},"body":{"extsrc":"[13] ⟩ [[Diple (textual symbol)|Diplé]]. Our copyists place this sign in the books of the people of the Church, to separate or to indicate the quotations drawn from the [[Holy Scripture]]s.\n"}}'><p id="mwNw">[13] ⟩ <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diple_(textual_symbol)" title="Diple (textual symbol)" id="mwOA">Diplé</a>. Our copyists place this sign in the books of the people of the Church, to separate or to indicate the quotations drawn from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Scripture" title="Holy Scripture" class="mw-redirect" id="mwOQ">Holy Scriptures</a>.</p></div></blockquote></div>
</div>

<p id="mwOg">The double quotation mark derives from a marginal notation used in fifteenth-century <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuscript" title="Manuscript" id="mwOw">manuscript</a> annotations to indicate a passage of particular importance (not necessarily a quotation); the notation was placed in the outside margin of the page and was repeated alongside each line of the passage.<sup about="#mwt57" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-guilles_7-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"guilles"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-guilles-7"}}'><a href="#cite_note-guilles-7" id="mwPA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwPQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwPg">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwPw">]</span></span></a></sup> In his edition of the works of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aristotle" title="Aristotle" id="mwQA">Aristotle</a>, which appeared in 1483 or 1484, the Milanese <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Renaissance_humanist" title="Renaissance humanist" class="mw-redirect" id="mwQQ">Renaissance humanist</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francesco_Filelfo" title="Francesco Filelfo" id="mwQg">Francesco Filelfo</a> marked literal and appropriate quotes with oblique double dashes on the left margin of each line.<sup about="#mwt58" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Castellani_8-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Castellani"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Castellani-8" id="mwQw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwRA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRQ">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRg">]</span></span></a></sup> Until then, literal quotations had been highlighted or not at the author's discretion.<sup about="#mwt59" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Castellani_8-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Castellani"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Castellani-8" id="mwRw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwSA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwSQ">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwSg">]</span></span></a></sup> <span class="cleanup-needed-content" style="padding-left:0.1em; padding-right:0.1em; color:var(--color-subtle, #54595d); border:1px solid var(--border-color-subtle, #c8ccd1);" about="#mwt54" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwSw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Clarify","href":"./Template:Clarify"},"params":{"text":{"wt":"Non-verbal loans"},"date":{"wt":"November 2022"}},"i":0}}]}'>Non-verbal loans</span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Wikipedia_articles_needing_clarification_from_November_2022" about="#mwt54"/><sup class="noprint Inline-Template " style="margin-left:0.1em; white-space:nowrap;" about="#mwt54" id="mwTA"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span><i><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Please_clarify" title="Wikipedia:Please clarify"><span title="The text near this tag may need clarification or removal of jargon. (November 2022)">clarification needed</span></a></i><span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></sup> were marked on the edge. After the publication of Filelfo's edition, the quotation marks for literal quotations prevailed.<sup about="#mwt62" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Castellani_8-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Castellani"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Castellani-8"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Castellani-8" id="mwTQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwTg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwTw">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUA">]</span></span></a></sup> During the seventeenth century this treatment became specific to quoted material, and it grew common, especially in Britain, to print quotation marks (now in the modern opening and closing forms) at the beginning and end of the quotation as well as in the margin; the French usage is a remnant of this. In most other languages, including English, the marginal marks dropped out of use in the last years of the eighteenth century. The usage of a pair of marks, opening and closing, at the level of lower case letters was generalized.<sup about="#mwt63" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-guilles_7-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"guilles"}}'><a href="#cite_note-guilles-7" id="mwUQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwUg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUw">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwVQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Guillemets_(Imprimerie_nationale,_1934).png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwVg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Guillemets_(Imprimerie_nationale,_1934).png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Guillemets_%28Imprimerie_nationale%2C_1934%29.png/250px-Guillemets_%28Imprimerie_nationale%2C_1934%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Guillemets_%28Imprimerie_nationale%2C_1934%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x" data-file-width="368" data-file-height="82" data-file-type="bitmap" height="56" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwVw"/></a><figcaption id="mwWA"><span title="French-language text" about="#mwt64" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwWQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"fr"},"2":{"wt":"Guillemets"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="fr">Guillemets</i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_French-language_text" about="#mwt64" id="mwWg"/> by the <span title="French-language text" about="#mwt65" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwWw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"fr"},"2":{"wt":"[[Imprimerie nationale]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="fr"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imprimerie_nationale" title="Imprimerie nationale">Imprimerie nationale</a></i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_French-language_text" about="#mwt65" id="mwXA"/> in <span title="French-language text" about="#mwt66" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwXQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang","href":"./Template:Lang"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"fr"},"2":{"wt":"Bulletin de l&apos;Agence générale des colonies"}},"i":0}}]}'><i lang="fr">Bulletin de l'Agence générale des colonies</i></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_French-language_text" about="#mwt66" id="mwXg"/>, No. 302, May 1934, showing the usage of a pair of marks, opening and closing, at the level of lower case letters</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwXw"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Quotation_&amp;_apostrophe.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwYA"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Quotation_&amp;_apostrophe.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Quotation_%26_apostrophe.svg/250px-Quotation_%26_apostrophe.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Quotation_%26_apostrophe.svg/500px-Quotation_%26_apostrophe.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="200" data-file-height="16" data-file-type="drawing" height="20" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwYQ"/></a><figcaption id="mwYg">Clash between the apostrophe and curved quotation marks in a phrase meaning "the crimes of the 'good Samaritans'<span style="white-space: nowrap;" about="#mwt67" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwYw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"thinsp","href":"./Template:Thinsp"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span>"</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwZA">By the nineteenth century, the design and usage began to be specific to each region. In <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Europe" title="Western Europe" id="mwZQ">Western Europe</a> the custom became to use the quotation mark pairs with the convexity of each mark aimed <i id="mwZg">outward.</i> In <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Britain" title="Great Britain" id="mwZw">Britain</a> those marks were elevated to the same height as the top of capital letters: <span class="serif-fonts" style="font-family: 'Georgia Pro', Georgia, 'DejaVu Serif', Times, 'Times New Roman', FreeSerif, 'DejaVu Math TeX', 'URW Bookman L', serif; " about="#mwt68" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwaA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"serif","href":"./Template:Serif"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"{{char|“...”}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;">“...”</span></span>.</p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwaQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Guillemets_&amp;_apostrophe.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwag"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Guillemets_&amp;_apostrophe.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Guillemets_%26_apostrophe.svg/250px-Guillemets_%26_apostrophe.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Guillemets_%26_apostrophe.svg/500px-Guillemets_%26_apostrophe.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="206" data-file-height="16" data-file-type="drawing" height="19" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwaw"/></a><figcaption id="mwbA">Clearly distinguishable apostrophe and angular quotation marks.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwbQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Quotation_&amp;_spaces.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwbg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Quotation_&amp;_spaces.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Quotation_%26_spaces.svg/250px-Quotation_%26_spaces.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Quotation_%26_spaces.svg/500px-Quotation_%26_spaces.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="585" data-file-height="190" data-file-type="drawing" height="81" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwbw"/></a><figcaption id="mwcA">Blank space (in yellow) provoked by elevated quotation marks; some type designers consider this excessive.<sup about="#mwt71" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-design_et_typo_9-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"design et typo"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-design_et_typo-9"}}'><a href="#cite_note-design_et_typo-9" id="mwcQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwcg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcw">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwdA">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwdQ">In <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Third_Republic" title="French Third Republic" id="mwdg">France</a>, by the end of the nineteenth century, the marks were modified to an angular shape. Some authors claim that the reason for this was a practical one, in order to get a character that was clearly distinguishable from apostrophes, commas, and parentheses. Also, in other scripts, the angular quotation marks are distinguishable from other punctuation characters: the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greek_diacritics#Breathings" title="Greek diacritics" id="mwdw">Greek breathing marks</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armenian_alphabet#Punctuation" title="Armenian alphabet" id="mweA">Armenian emphasis and apostrophe</a>, the Arabic <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comma#In_other_languages" title="Comma" id="mweQ">comma</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decimal_mark#Other_numeral_systems" title="Decimal mark" class="mw-redirect" id="mweg">decimal separator</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thousands_separator" title="Thousands separator" class="mw-redirect" id="mwew">thousands separator</a>, etc. Other authors<sup about="#mwt73" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-design_et_typo_9-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"design et typo"}}'><a href="#cite_note-design_et_typo-9" id="mwfA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwfQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfg">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfw">]</span></span></a></sup><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_with_specifically_marked_weasel-worded_phrases" about="#mwt72" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwgA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Who","href":"./Template:Who"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"August 2025"}},"i":0}}]}'/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_with_specifically_marked_weasel-worded_phrases_from_August_2025" about="#mwt72"/><sup class="noprint Inline-Template " style="white-space:nowrap;" about="#mwt72" id="mwgQ"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span><i><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Manual_of_Style/Words_to_watch#Unsupported_attributions" title="Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Words to watch"><span title="The material near this tag possibly uses too-vague attribution or weasel words. (August 2025)">who?</span></a></i><span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></sup> claim that the reason for this was an aesthetic one: the elevated quotation marks created extra white space before and after the word, below the quotation marks. This was considered aesthetically unpleasing, while the in-line quotation marks helped to maintain the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_color" title="Type color" id="mwgg">typographical color</a>, since the quotation marks had the same height and were aligned with the lower case letters.<sup about="#mwt74" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-guilles_7-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"guilles"}}'><a href="#cite_note-guilles-7" id="mwgw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwhA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhQ">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhg">]</span></span></a></sup> Nevertheless, while other languages do not insert spaces between the quotation marks and the word(s) quoted, the French usage does insert them, even if they are narrow spaces.</p>

<p id="mwhw">The <i id="mwiA">curved</i> quotation marks ("66–99") usage, <span class="serif-fonts" style="font-family: 'Georgia Pro', Georgia, 'DejaVu Serif', Times, 'Times New Roman', FreeSerif, 'DejaVu Math TeX', 'URW Bookman L', serif; " about="#mwt75" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwiQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"serif","href":"./Template:Serif"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"{{char|“...”}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;">“...”</span></span>, was exported to some non-Latin scripts, notably where there was some <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_language" title="English language" id="mwig">English</a> influence, for instance in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous_languages_of_the_Americas" title="Indigenous languages of the Americas" id="mwiw">Native American</a> scripts and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahmic_scripts" title="Brahmic scripts" id="mwjA">Indic scripts</a>.<sup about="#mwt81" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-10" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-10"}}'><a href="#cite_note-10" id="mwjQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwjg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjw">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt84" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Deccan-Kelkar_11-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Deccan-Kelkar"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Deccan-Kelkar-11"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Deccan-Kelkar-11" id="mwkQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwkg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkw">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwlA">]</span></span></a></sup> <!-- See [[{{TALKPAGENAME}}#Indic quotation mark direction]] -->On the other hand, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greek_script" title="Greek script" class="mw-redirect" id="mwlQ">Greek</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyrillic_script" title="Cyrillic script" id="mwlg">Cyrillic</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arabic_script" title="Arabic script" id="mwlw">Arabic</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethiopic_script" title="Ethiopic script" class="mw-redirect" id="mwmA">Ethiopic</a> adopted the French "angular" quotation marks, <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt76" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwmQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"«...»"}},"i":0}}]}'>«...»</span>.<link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_with_unsourced_statements" about="#mwt77" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwmg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"citation needed","href":"./Template:Citation_needed"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"June 2020"}},"i":0}}]}'/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_with_unsourced_statements_from_June_2020" about="#mwt77"/><sup class="noprint Inline-Template Template-Fact" style="white-space:nowrap;" about="#mwt77" id="mwmw"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span><i><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed" title="Wikipedia:Citation needed"><span title="This claim needs references to reliable sources. (June 2020)">citation needed</span></a></i><span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></sup> The angle bracket quotation marks, <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt78" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwnA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"《...》"}},"i":0}}]}'>《...》</span>, are also a development of the in-line angular quotation marks. These marks are standard in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanzi" title="Hanzi" class="mw-redirect" id="mwnQ">Hanzi</a> (Chinese 汉字), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanja" title="Hanja" id="mwng">Hanja</a> (Korean 漢字), and modern <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hangul" title="Hangul" id="mwnw">Hangul</a> (Korean 한글) scripts. In Chinese, they serve as the standard punctuation for book, album, and other work titles. In South Korea, they are recognized as valid punctuation for indicating such titles. In North Korea, they serve as the standard punctuation for book, album, and other work titles.<sup about="#mwt87" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-12" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-12"}}'><a href="#cite_note-12" id="mwoA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwoQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwog">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwow">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt90" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-13" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-13"}}'><a href="#cite_note-13" id="mwpA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwpQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwpg">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwpw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt93" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-14" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-14"}}'><a href="#cite_note-14" id="mwqA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwqQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqg">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwrA">In <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Europe" title="Central Europe" id="mwrQ">Central Europe</a>, the practice was to use the quotation mark pairs with the convexity aimed <i id="mwrg">inward.</i> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_language" title="German language" id="mwrw">German</a> tradition preferred the curved quotation marks, the first one at the level of the commas, the second one at the level of the apostrophes: <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt94" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwsA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"„...“"}},"i":0}}]}'>„...“</span>. Alternatively, these marks could be angular and in-line with lower case letters, but still pointing inward: <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt95" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwsQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"»...«"}},"i":0}}]}'>»...«</span>. Some neighboring regions adopted the German curved marks tradition with lower–upper alignment, while some, e.g. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poland" title="Poland" id="mwsg">Poland</a>, adopted a variant with the convexity of the closing mark aimed rightward like the opening one, <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt96" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwsw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"„...”"}},"i":0}}]}'>„...”</span>.<sup about="#mwt99" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-15" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-15"}}'><a href="#cite_note-15" id="mwtA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwtQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtg">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwuA"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweden" title="Sweden" id="mwuQ">Sweden</a> (and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finland" title="Finland" id="mwug">Finland</a>) chose a convention where the convexity of both marks was aimed to the right but lined up both at the top level: <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt100" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwuw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"”...”"}},"i":0}}]}'>”...”</span>.</p>

<p id="mwvA">In <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Europe" title="Eastern Europe" id="mwvQ">Eastern Europe</a>,<link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Wikipedia_articles_needing_clarification_from_December_2021" about="#mwt101" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwvg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"clarify","href":"./Template:Clarify"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"December 2021"}},"i":0}}]}'/><sup class="noprint Inline-Template " style="margin-left:0.1em; white-space:nowrap;" about="#mwt101" id="mwvw"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span><i><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Please_clarify" title="Wikipedia:Please clarify"><span title="The text near this tag may need clarification or removal of jargon. (December 2021)">clarification needed</span></a></i><span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></sup> there was hesitation between the French tradition <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt102" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwwA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"«...»"}},"i":0}}]}'>«...»</span> and the German tradition <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt103" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwwQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"„...“"}},"i":0}}]}'>„...“</span>. The French tradition prevailed in Eastern Europe (Russia, Ukraine, and Belarus), whereas the German tradition, or its modified version with the convexity of the closing mark aimed rightward, has become dominant in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southeastern_Europe" title="Southeastern Europe" id="mwwg">Southeastern Europe</a>, e.g. in the Balkan countries. In <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romania" title="Romania" id="mwww">Romania</a> the: <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt104" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwxA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"„...”"}},"i":0}}]}'>„...”</span> version is officially recognized by the Romanian Academy.</p>

<p id="mwxQ">In some languages using the angular quotation marks, the usage of the single guillemet, <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt105" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwxg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"‹...›"}},"i":0}}]}'>‹...›</span>, became obsolete, being replaced by double curved ones: <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt106" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwxw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"“...”"}},"i":0}}]}'>“...”</span>, though the single ones still survive, for instance, in Switzerland. In Russia, Ukraine and Belarus, the curved quotation marks, <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt107" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwyA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"„...“"}},"i":0}}]}'>„...“</span>, are used as a secondary level or in handwriting, while the angular marks, <span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;" about="#mwt108" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwyQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"char","href":"./Template:Char"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"«...»"}},"i":0}}]}'>«...»</span>, are used as the primary level on printed text.</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwyg" aria-labelledby="In_English"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="In_English">In English<span class="anchor" id="Quotation_marks_in_English"></span></h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Quotation_mark&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2" title="Edit section: In English"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwyw"><span class="anchor" id="Curly_quotes" about="#mwt109" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"anchor","href":"./Template:Anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Curly quotes"}},"i":0}}]}'></span></p><!-- [[Curly quotes]] redirects to this section; please update it if you change the header. -->
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt110" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwzA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Quotation marks in English"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt111" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt110" id="mwzQ">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quotation_marks_in_English" title="Quotation marks in English">Quotation marks in English</a></div>
<p id="mwzg">In English writing, quotation marks are placed in pairs around a word or phrase to indicate:</p>
<ul id="mwzw"><li id="mw0A"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quotation" title="Quotation" id="mw0Q">Quotation</a> or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct_speech" title="Direct speech" class="mw-redirect" id="mw0g">direct speech</a>: <span class="example" style="font-family: Georgia, 'DejaVu Serif', serif; color: #006F00;" about="#mwt112" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw0w" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"xt3","href":"./Template:Xt3"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Carol said \"Go ahead\" when I asked her if the launcher was ready."}},"i":0}}]}'>Carol said "Go ahead" when I asked her if the launcher was ready.</span></li>
<li id="mw1A">Mention in another work of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Title_(publishing)" title="Title (publishing)" id="mw1Q">title</a> of a short or subsidiary work, such as a chapter or an episode: <span class="example" style="font-family: Georgia, 'DejaVu Serif', serif; color: #006F00;" about="#mwt113" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw1g" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"xt3","href":"./Template:Xt3"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"\"Encounter at Farpoint\" was the pilot episode of &apos;&apos;Star Trek: The Next Generation&apos;&apos;"}},"i":0}}]}'>"Encounter at Farpoint" was the pilot episode of <i>Star Trek: The Next Generation</i></span>.</li>
<li id="mw1w"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scare_quotes" title="Scare quotes" id="mw2A">Scare quotes</a>, used to mean "<a rel="mw:WikiLink/Interwiki" href="https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/so-called" title="wikt:so-called" class="extiw" id="mw2Q">so-called</a>" or to express <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarcasm" title="Sarcasm" id="mw2g">sarcasm</a>: <span class="example" style="font-family: Georgia, 'DejaVu Serif', serif; color: #006F00;" about="#mwt114" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw2w" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"xt3","href":"./Template:Xt3"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"The \"fresh\" bread was all dried up"}},"i":0}}]}'>The "fresh" bread was all dried up</span>.</li></ul>

<p id="mw3A">In American writing, quotation marks are normally the double kind (the primary style). If quotation marks are used inside another pair of quotation marks, then single quotation marks are used. For example: <span class="example" style="font-family: Georgia, 'DejaVu Serif', serif; color: #006F00;" about="#mwt115" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw3Q" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"xt3","href":"./Template:Xt3"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"\"Didn&apos;t she say &apos;I like red best&apos; when I asked her wine preferences?\" he asked his guests."}},"i":0}}]}'>"Didn't she say 'I like red best' when I asked her wine preferences?" he asked his guests.</span> If another set of quotation marks is nested inside single quotation marks, double quotation marks are used again, and they continue to alternate as necessary (though this is rarely done).</p>

<p id="mw3g">British publishing is regarded as more flexible about whether double or single quotation marks should be used.<sup about="#mwt118" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Walker_16-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Walker"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Walker-16"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Walker-16" id="mw3w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw4A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4Q">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4g">]</span></span></a></sup> A tendency to use single quotation marks in British writing is thought to have arisen after the mid-19th century invention of steam-powered presses and the consequent rise of London and New York as distinct, industrialized publishing centers whose publishing houses adhered to separate norms.<sup about="#mwt121" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-17" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-17"}}'><a href="#cite_note-17" id="mw4w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw5A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5Q">[</span>16<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5g">]</span></span></a></sup> <i id="mw5w"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_King's_English" title="The King's English" id="mw6A">The King's English</a></i> in 1908 noted that the prevailing British practice was to use double marks for most purposes, and single ones for quotations within quotations.<sup about="#mwt124" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-18" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-18"}}'><a href="#cite_note-18" id="mw6Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw6g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw6w">[</span>17<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw7A">]</span></span></a></sup> Different media now follow different conventions in the United Kingdom.</p>

<p id="mw7Q"><span class="anchor" id="Logical" about="#mwt125" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"anchor","href":"./Template:Anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Logical"}},"i":0}}]}'></span>Different varieties and styles of English have different conventions regarding whether terminal punctuation should be written <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logical_quotation" title="Logical quotation" class="mw-redirect" id="mw7g">inside or outside</a> the quotation marks. North American printing usually puts full stops and commas (but not colons, semicolons, exclamation or question marks) inside the closing quotation mark, whether it is part of the original quoted material or not.<sup about="#mwt128" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-19" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-19"}}'><a href="#cite_note-19" id="mw7w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw8A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8Q">[</span>18<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8g">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt131" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-20" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-20"}}'><a href="#cite_note-20" id="mw8w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw9A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw9Q">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw9g">]</span></span></a></sup> Styles elsewhere vary widely and have different rationales for placing it inside or outside, often a matter of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Style_guide" title="Style guide" id="mw9w">house style</a>.</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="3" id="mw-A" aria-labelledby="Typographic_forms"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Typographic_forms">Typographic forms<span class="anchor" id="Curly"></span></h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Quotation_mark&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3" title="Edit section: Typographic forms"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt132" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw-Q" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"see also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Typographic approximation"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt133" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt132" id="mw-g">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Typographic_approximation" title="Typographic approximation">Typographic approximation</a></div>
<p id="mw-w">Regarding their appearance, two shape classifications of quotation marks are used in English-language texts:</p>

<ul id="mw_A"><li id="mw_Q"><span class="serif-fonts" style="font-family: 'Georgia Pro', Georgia, 'DejaVu Serif', Times, 'Times New Roman', FreeSerif, 'DejaVu Math TeX', 'URW Bookman L', serif; " about="#mwt134" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw_g" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"serif","href":"./Template:Serif"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"{{large|{{char|&apos;...&apos;}}}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><span style="font-size:120%"><span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;">'...'</span></span></span> and <span class="serif-fonts" style="font-family: 'Georgia Pro', Georgia, 'DejaVu Serif', Times, 'Times New Roman', FreeSerif, 'DejaVu Math TeX', 'URW Bookman L', serif; " about="#mwt135" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw_w" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"serif","href":"./Template:Serif"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"{{large|{{char|\"...\"}}}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><span style="font-size:120%"><span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;">"..."</span></span></span> are known as neutral, vertical, straight, typewriter, dumb, or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASCII" title="ASCII" id="mwAQA">ASCII</a> quotation marks. The left and right marks are identical. These are the symbols found on typical <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/QWERTY" title="QWERTY" id="mwAQE">QWERTY</a> keyboards, although they are sometimes <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autocorrection" title="Autocorrection" id="mwAQI">automatically converted</a> to the other type by software.</li>
<li id="mwAQM"><span class="serif-fonts" style="font-family: 'Georgia Pro', Georgia, 'DejaVu Serif', Times, 'Times New Roman', FreeSerif, 'DejaVu Math TeX', 'URW Bookman L', serif; " about="#mwt136" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAQQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"serif","href":"./Template:Serif"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"{{large|{{char|‘...’}}}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><span style="font-size:120%"><span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;">‘...’</span></span></span> and <span class="serif-fonts" style="font-family: 'Georgia Pro', Georgia, 'DejaVu Serif', Times, 'Times New Roman', FreeSerif, 'DejaVu Math TeX', 'URW Bookman L', serif; " about="#mwt137" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAQU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"serif","href":"./Template:Serif"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"{{large|{{char|“...”}}}}"}},"i":0}}]}'><span style="font-size:120%"><span class="nounderlines" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color-muted,#ddd); color: var(--color-base,#202122); background-color: var( --background-color-neutral-subtle, #fdfdfd); padding: 1px 1px;">“...”</span></span></span> are known as typographic, curly, curved, book, or smart quotation marks. (The doubled ones are more informally known as "66 and 99".<sup about="#mwt140" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-21" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-21"}}'><a href="#cite_note-21" id="mwAQY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQg">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt143" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-22" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-22"}}'><a href="#cite_note-22" id="mwAQo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQw">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQ0">]</span></span></a></sup>) The beginning marks look like commas raised to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ascender_(typography)" title="Ascender (typography)" id="mwAQ4">ascender line</a> and rotated 180<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAQ8"> </span>degrees (and thus known as '<b id="mwARA">inverted commas'</b>). The ending marks look like commas raised to the ascender line. Curved quotation marks are used mainly in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuscript" title="Manuscript" id="mwARE">manuscript</a>, printing, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Typesetting" title="Typesetting" id="mwARI">typesetting</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_case" title="Type case" id="mwARM">Type cases</a> (of any language) generally have the curved quotation mark metal types for the respective language, and may lack the vertical quotation mark metal types. Because most computer keyboards lack keys to enter typographic quotation marks directly, much that is written using <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_processor_program" title="Word processor program" class="mw-redirect" id="mwARQ">word-processing programs</a> has vertical quotation marks. The "<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smart_quotes" title="Smart quotes" class="mw-redirect" id="mwARU">smart quotes</a>" feature in some computer software can convert vertical quotation marks to curly ones, although sometimes inappropriately.</li></ul>

<p id="mwARY">The closing or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_single_quotation_mark" title="Right single quotation mark" id="mwARc">right single quotation mark</a> is identical in form to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apostrophe" title="Apostrophe" id="mwARg">apostrophe</a> and similar to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_(symbol)" title="Prime (symbol)" id="mwARk">prime</a> symbol. The double quotation mark is identical to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ditto_mark" title="Ditto mark" id="mwARo">ditto mark</a> in English-language usage. It is also similar to—and often used to represent—the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_prime" title="Double prime" class="mw-redirect" id="mwARs">double prime</a> symbol. These all serve different purposes and have distinct Unicode <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Codepoint" title="Codepoint" class="mw-redirect" id="mwARw">codepoints</a>.</p>

<ul class="gallery mw-gallery-traditional" typeof="mw:Extension/gallery" about="#mwt144" id="mwAR0" data-mw='{"name":"gallery","attrs":{"widths":"200px","heights":"155px"},"body":{}}'>
<li class="gallerybox" style="width: 235px;" id="mwAR4"><div class="thumb" style="width: 230px; height: 185px;" id="mwAR8"><span typeof="mw:File" id="mwASA"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Underwoodfive.jpg" class="mw-file-description" title="A typewriter" id="mwASE"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Underwoodfive.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Underwoodfive.jpg/250px-Underwoodfive.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Underwoodfive.jpg/500px-Underwoodfive.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="A typewriter" data-file-width="640" data-file-height="480" data-file-type="bitmap" height="150" width="200" class="mw-file-element" id="mwASI"/></a></span></div><div class="gallerytext" id="mwASM">A <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Typewriter" title="Typewriter" id="mwASQ">typewriter</a></div></li>
<li class="gallerybox" style="width: 235px;" id="mwASU"><div class="thumb" style="width: 230px; height: 185px;" id="mwASY"><span typeof="mw:File" id="mwASc"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Upper_case_and_lower_case_types.jpg" class="mw-file-description" title="A type case containing sorts of movable type" id="mwASg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Upper_case_and_lower_case_types.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Upper_case_and_lower_case_types.jpg/250px-Upper_case_and_lower_case_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Upper_case_and_lower_case_types.jpg/500px-Upper_case_and_lower_case_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="A type case containing sorts of movable type" data-file-width="1000" data-file-height="750" data-file-type="bitmap" height="150" width="200" class="mw-file-element" id="mwASk"/></a></span></div><div class="gallerytext" id="mwASo">A <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_case" title="Type case" id="mwASs">type case</a> containing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sort_(typesetting)" title="Sort (typesetting)" id="mwASw">sorts</a> of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Movable_type" title="Movable type" id="mwAS0">movable type</a> </div></li>
<li class="gallerybox" style="width: 235px;" id="mwAS4"><div class="thumb" style="width: 230px; height: 185px;" id="mwAS8"><span typeof="mw:File" id="mwATA"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:LenovoKeyboard.jpg" class="mw-file-description" title="A wired computer keyboard for desktop use" id="mwATE"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:LenovoKeyboard.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/LenovoKeyboard.jpg/250px-LenovoKeyboard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/LenovoKeyboard.jpg/500px-LenovoKeyboard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="A wired computer keyboard for desktop use" data-file-width="4928" data-file-height="3264" data-file-type="bitmap" height="132" width="200" class="mw-file-element" id="mwATI"/></a></span></div><div class="gallerytext" id="mwATM">A wired <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_keyboard" title="Computer keyboard" id="mwATQ">computer keyboard</a> for desktop use</div></li>
</ul>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mwATU" aria-labelledby="Summary_table"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Summary_table">Summary table</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Quotation_mark&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4" title="Edit section: Summary table"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwATY">Other languages have similar conventions to English, but use different symbols or different placement.</p>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt145" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwATc" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sticky header","href":"./Template:Sticky_header"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1305414688" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt146" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"template:sticky header/styles.css"}}'>@media screen and (min-width:640px){.mw-parser-output .sticky-header>thead>tr:first-child,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header>caption+tbody>tr:first-child,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header>tbody:first-child>tr:first-child,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header-multi>thead{position:sticky;top:0;z-index:10}body.skin-timeless .mw-parser-output .content-table-wrapper.overflowed .sticky-header>thead>tr:first-child,body.skin-timeless .mw-parser-output .content-table-wrapper.overflowed .sticky-header>caption+tbody>tr:first-child,body.skin-timeless .mw-parser-output .content-table-wrapper.overflowed .sticky-header>tbody:first-child>tr:first-child,body.skin-timeless .mw-parser-output .content-table-wrapper.overflowed .sticky-header-multi>thead{position:static}.mw-parser-output .sticky-header:not(.wikitable),.mw-parser-output .sticky-header-multi:not(.wikitable){background-color:var(--color-inverted,#fff)}.mw-parser-output .sticky-header:not(.wikitable)>*,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header:not(.wikitable)>thead>tr:first-child,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header:not(.wikitable)>caption+tbody>tr:first-child,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header:not(.wikitable)>tbody:first-child>tr:first-child,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header-multi:not(.wikitable)>thead,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header-multi>thead{background-color:inherit}.mw-parser-output .sticky-header.wikitable,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header-multi.wikitable{border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:0;border-width:0 1px 1px 0}.mw-parser-output .sticky-header.wikitable td,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header.wikitable th,.mw-parser-output .sticky-header-multi.wi